This is infrastructure for regression testing the Ott implementation (by FZN, but this README is much later by PS; it might be wrong).

`./regression`, from `regression.ml`, runs Ott on a set of tests and runs Coq, Isabelle, HOL4, Lem, Lean, OCaml, and Latex on the generated code.  For some tests, not all of those are intended to work, so the harness does two separate jobs, and each is a separate invocation.

Running the tests writes a report:

```
$ ./regression -run report.txt -todo_list
```

Comparing two such reports shows what changed:

```
$ ./regression -compare report-2026-09-12.txt report.txt
```

`./regression -help` shows the command-line options.


REPORTS
=======

A report is a plain text file.  It records, for each test and each tool, two characters: whether Ott generated the output for that tool, and whether the tool then accepted it.

```
+  succeeded
-  failed
s  skipped, by a -no_<tool> flag or by the config file
.  not reached, because the step before it failed
```

So `++` is a clean pass, `+-` means Ott generated output that the tool rejected, and `-.` means Ott declined to generate and so the tool never ran.  `-.` is common and is not in itself a problem: Ott refuses to generate output for a source file whose meta or sugar productions lack a hom for that target, so a test with no `lean` homs shows `-.` in the Lean column.

The `# tools` line names the columns, so a report written before a backend existed can still be compared against one written after; the comparison uses the columns they have in common and says which columns are in only one of them.

```
# ott-regression-report 1
# date 2026-09-12T12:02:50
# ott Ott version 0.34   distribution of Mon Dec 30 10:12:45 GMT 2024
# tools Coq CoqNL Isa HOL Lem Lean OCaml LaTeX

test                   Coq CoqNL  Isa  HOL  Lem Lean OCaml LaTeX
../tests/test10.ott     ++    ++   ss   ss   ++   -.    ++    ss
../tests/test10st.ott   ++    ++   ss   ss   ++   ++    -.    ss

totals                 Coq CoqNL  Isa  HOL  Lem Lean OCaml LaTeX   all
generated                2     2    0    0    2    1     1     0     8
ott-failed               0     0    0    0    0    1     1     0     2
passed                   2     2    0    0    2    1     1     0     8
failed                   0     0    0    0    0    0     0     0     0
skipped                  0     0    2    2    0    0     0     2     6
```

The totals at the foot count each outcome per tool, with an `all` column summing across tools.


COMPARISONS
===========

A comparison lists each test whose results changed, and under it only the tools whose cell differs.  A tool going from failing to passing is a progression, the reverse is a regression, and anything else, such as a tool becoming skipped, is just a change.  Tests present in only one of the two reports are called out.  The same shape of totals block goes at the foot.

```
*** comparison
*** A report-2026-09-12.txt   2026-09-12T10:00:00
*** B report.txt              2026-09-12T12:02:50

../tests/test10.ott    CoqNL  +- -> ++  progression
                       Lean   ++ -> -.  regression
../tests/test5.ott     only in B

totals                 Coq CoqNL  Isa  HOL  Lem Lean OCaml LaTeX   all
progressions             0     1    0    0    0    0     0     0     1
regressions              0     0    0    0    0    1     0     0     1
changes                  0     0    0    0    0    0     0     0     0
```


THE CONFIG FILE
===============

`config.otr`, used with `-use_config`, says which tools to run for which tests.  It names its columns in a `# tools` header rather than relying on position, so adding or reordering a backend does not silently change what every existing line means.  `+` runs that tool, `.` skips it and records `ss`.


PROVERS
=======

The provers are not part of this repository.  `install-provers.sh` installs Rocq, HOL4, Isabelle, Lean, and Lem; run `install-provers-prerequisites.sh` first.  The Lem check runs `lem` with no backend flag, which typechecks without writing output, and the Lean check runs `lean` on the generated file.


OLDER BASELINES
===============

Before this was reorganised, runs wrote a binary `.bl` file via `Marshal` and the comparison was folded into the run.  The `.bl` files can no longer be read, and the `.txt` dumps beside them (`baseline-2022-03.txt`, `baseline-2026-09-12.txt`) are in the older layout, where a skipped tool was rendered as blanks.  They are kept as historical artifacts to read by eye.  `-compare` will not parse them.
